 But we're actually beginning to see some applications.

105
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:50,720
 And that's something that if you haven't been paying attention

106
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:52,920
 to crypto, you might not realize.

107
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:53,760
 - Totally.

108
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,800
 And I think the results speak for themselves, right?

109
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:58,000
 Active addresses across all of the blockchains

110
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,880
 that we tracked, hit an all-time high,

111
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:03,920
 this last month of 220 million mobile wallet users.

112
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,920
 So data that we get from mobile app stores

113
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:10,200
 that also recently hit an all-time high of 27 million.

114
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:13,640
 And so the numbers do kind of speak to this idea

115
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:17,240
 that we are now seeing some of these breakout applications

116
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:22,160
 that are bringing real on-chain transactions and usage.

117
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:23,320
 - Let's go through each of those.

118
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:26,440
 You mentioned that active addresses are at an all-time high.

119
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:30,560
 Now, this is a kind of controversial metric.

120
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:34,200
 Not everybody loves talking about active addresses.

121
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:35,680
 One of the things they have going for them

122
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:38,000
 is that they are easy to measure.

123
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,720
 It's something that you can very easily quantify.

124
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:44,800
 - I think to really understand why active addresses

125
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,400
 has become such a hot topic recently,

126
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:52,840
 I think we need to look at some of the historical context.

127
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:54,440
 I think in traditional software,

128
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:57,840
 most people tend to understand the basic concept of a user.

129
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:01,280
 Of course, there's ways to measure the quality of the user.

130
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:04,160
 There's a whole field of growth analytics around this topic.

131
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,040
 But in its most simple form,

132
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:09,440
 I think people can grok the idea of a daily active user

133
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:10,560
 or a monthly active user.

134
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:15,080
 It's just the number of people that are using your product

135
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:17,240
 in service on a daily or monthly basis.

136
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,120
 It's fairly straightforward.

137
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:22,280
 But in crypto, things are a lot trickier.

138
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,960
 And the reason is because on blockchains,

139
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,440
 user identities are pseudonymous.

140
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:30,480
 This means that it's very easy for one person to create

141
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:33,960
 and control a group of public addresses or identities.

142
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:37,920
 This is called a Sible. And up until recently,

143
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:41,760
 a lot of the most popular blockchains had very limited capacity,

144
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:43,840
 which resulted in high transaction fees,

145
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:46,160
 which created this natural barrier

146
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:51,320
 or spinning up and using thousands or hundreds of different addresses

147
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:54,400
 because it would come with significant monetary costs.

148
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:57,760
 But recently, we've seen that crypto infrastructure

149
00:06:57,760 --> 00:07:01,120
 has become more scalable via L2s

150
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:03,120
 and new high throughput layer ones,

151
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,880
 which has reduced the cost of transactions

152
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,120
 on many of these blockchains to nearly zero.

153
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:10,600
 And maybe you might ask,

154
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,840
 isn't it also near zero costs to create multiple identities

155
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,360
 on traditional internet applications?

156
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:19,840
 And I'd say, yes, that's true for the most part.

157
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:23,320
 I can create multiple email addresses pretty easily.

158
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:26,360
 But the key difference here is that in the crypto world,

159
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,960
 we have very strong incentives for this type of behavior.

160
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:31,640
 - Yeah, Google doesn't give you money

161
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:34,240
 for opening a new Gmail account, unfortunately.

162
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:35,160
 - Exactly.

163
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:36,960
 And a lot of new tokens today

164
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:39,320
 actually bootstrapped their circulating supply

165
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:41,000
 by doing something called an airdrop

166
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,080
 where you're rewarding retroactively users of the protocol

167
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:47,640
 based on a predefined set of addresses.

168
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:49,880
 And so as long as people believe

169
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,720
 that these tokens could be valuable,

170
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,840
 there is a strong incentive for them to try to game the system

171
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:59,480
 by creating and transacting under many different identities.

172
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:02,120
 This is commonly known as airdrop farming.

173
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:04,400
 - Yeah, there's no reason that one person

174
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:07,960
 couldn't spin up hundreds of millions of addresses

175
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:11,920
 and just go wild engaging in totally useless

176
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:15,240
 and inauthentic activity in order to try to maximize

177
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:19,520
 their likelihood of extracting as many rewards as possible.

178
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:22,600
 And what we've seen over the last four or five years

179
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:27,440
 that we've had airdrops and yield farms and so on,

180
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:30,680
 there's been a profound professionalization

181
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:34,800
 of the efforts to pull out those rewards.

182
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:37,160
 People have gotten really, really, really good.

183
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:39,560
 And I wanna make it really clear to anybody

184
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:42,040
 who thinks that it is a giga brain take

185
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:46,000
 to say monthly active addresses is simpleable.

186
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,200
 Yes, we know that.

187
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:52,200
 And if you have a better way to measure using public data,

188
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:55,800
 that's verifiable by everyone or that is easy to trust,

189
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:59,400
 I'd love to hear it, tell me a better way.

190
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:03,160
 I'm thirsty for it, tell me, let's work together

191
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:05,680
 and let's figure it out because the space deserves

192
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,600
 really high quality transparent metrics.

193
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:09,960
 And that's what we're trying to do.

194
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:12,920
 - Yeah, and so given what we know about these behaviors,

195
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:14,680
 how many users are there, right?

196
00:09:14,680 --> 00:09:17,520
 Is it 10 million, is it 50 million, is it 100 million?

197
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:19,840
 This is the question that we've put a lot of work in

198
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,640
 and we're going to publish some of our thoughts

199
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:24,600
 around estimating that.

200
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:26,400
 And we'll include that blog post in the show notes,

201
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:29,240
 but maybe you could walk us through how you think

202
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:31,880
 about actually getting from this big number,

203
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:34,520
 this 220 million number, if that's the number of addresses,

204
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:36,120
 how do we get down to humans?

205
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:38,280
 How do we reduce that to the number of users,

206
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:43,200
 the actual flesh and bones on the other end of the screen?

207
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:45,720
 - I think one approach sounds kind of obvious,

208
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:49,880
 which is we've got these 220 million monthly active addresses.

209
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:54,280
 What if we could filter out what we suspect are bots

210
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:56,000
 and sibbles?

211
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,320
 And the way that we can do this is some techniques

212
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:01,840
 using on-chain analytics and forensics.

213
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:06,840
 One method is to filter out addresses that received funds

214
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:10,120
 from something called a dispersion contract.

215
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:12,680
 This is a smart contract whose sole purpose

216
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:15,560
 is to take in funds and automatically distribute them

217
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:17,480
 across many different addresses.

218
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:19,720
 Of course, there could be false positives here,

219
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:23,640
 but the activity implies that the destination addresses

220
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:25,880
 all received funds from a single source

221
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:28,200
 and therefore they're connected in some way.

222
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:30,960
 So there's certain types of filtering you can do

223
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:33,760
 around dispersion contracts specifically

224
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:37,480
 that can eliminate a lot of the bots that you might see.

225
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:41,040
 The other approach involves looking at addresses

226
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:44,520
 that have a near zero balance at both the beginning

227
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:47,720
 and end of the period that you're aggregating over.

228
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:51,280
 For example, if you're trying to find the real number

229
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,920
 of monthly active users in September,

230
00:10:53,920 --> 00:10:56,760
 you can look at addresses that had a zero balance

231
00:10:56,760 --> 00:10:59,880
 on both September 1st and September 30th.

232
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:02,560
 This criteria implies that the addresses

233
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:04,840
 were transient in nature.

234
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:08,360
 I think bots and sibbles typically want to clean up

235
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:11,320
 their balances after taking actions on-chain,

236
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:14,920
 whereas real human users usually like to keep some balance

237
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:17,840
 in their wallets to cover future transaction fees.

238
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:19,520
 So this is like an address popped up

239
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,880
 and then within like some period of time,

240
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:23,520
 people dumped into another thing

241
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:26,040
 and the address never was revived after that.

242
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:27,760
 - Exactly, this is the type of behavior

243
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:29,600
 where it received a source of funds,

244
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:32,600
 it took some action and then it pushed all of those funds

245
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:35,480
 outside so it's a very transient type of activity.

246
00:11:35,480 --> 00:11:38,640
 That's often associated with bots and sibbles.

247
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:40,080
 The other thing that we've looked into

248
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:42,880
 is just like the frequency of transactions

249
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:45,520
 and so you can analyze these distributions

250
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:48,120
 to look at did the address transact one time,

251
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:50,080
 two times, three times, four times,

252
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:53,160
 five or more times during the period addresses

253
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:56,160
 that have just one or two transactions.

254
00:11:56,160 --> 00:12:00,000
 At best they're a low quality user, at worst they're a bot

255
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,640
 or a sibble and especially over longer periods of time.

256
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:05,560
 This type of analysis can help you

257
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:07,480
 in some of that filtering.

258
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:10,120
 You can also specifically look at addresses

259
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:12,200
 that transacted very, very frequently

260
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:14,000
 over very short periods of time.

261
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,480
