00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:58,840
 across blockchains, geographies, technologies and categories.

524
00:23:58,840 --> 00:23:59,840
 And we've done that.

525
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:02,360
 We've made actually a dashboard that we call

526
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:04,520
 the builder energy dashboard.

527
00:24:04,520 --> 00:24:09,120
 It has some very cool interactive data visualizations

528
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:10,960
 around all this data.

529
00:24:10,960 --> 00:24:15,320
 And you can check it out at builderenergy.a16zcrypto.com.

530
00:24:15,320 --> 00:24:17,960
 - One thing I'll point out is we were talking about

531
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:20,160
 blockchains that have the most active addresses.

532
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:22,400
 And it's interesting to me that it sort of lines up

533
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:25,480
 with where builders are interested in building on

534
00:24:25,480 --> 00:24:27,000
 and what they are building on.

535
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:31,920
 We've seen more and more builders interested in Solana.

536
00:24:31,920 --> 00:24:34,080
 And actually building projects on Solana.

537
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:36,720
 That was the biggest jump in interest

538
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:41,600
 that any blockchain had seen between this year and last year.

539
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:44,040
 Base as well, super high up there.

540
00:24:44,040 --> 00:24:46,120
 And even Bitcoin in slot number three

541
00:24:46,120 --> 00:24:48,880
 had a really big bump in people who want to build on it.

542
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:51,920
 - Yep. And maybe just to add one clarifying point here,

543
00:24:51,920 --> 00:24:54,840
 the way that we source this data comes from a question

544
00:24:54,840 --> 00:24:56,880
 that we ask founders and builders.

545
00:24:56,880 --> 00:24:59,800
 We ask them simply what blockchain ecosystems

546
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:01,400
 would you be interested in building on?

547
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:04,280
 So it does not mean that there are this many builders

548
00:25:04,280 --> 00:25:06,200
 specifically deploying contracts

549
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:08,640
 or that there are a certain number of protocols

550
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:09,560
 on these blockchains.

551
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:12,600
 It simply just means kind of where generally speaking

552
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:15,440
 are these builders interested in building on.

553
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:20,280
 And I'd say still today, Ethereum and all of the layer twos,

554
00:25:20,280 --> 00:25:24,480
 including base and arbitrum and optimism and ZK sync

555
00:25:24,480 --> 00:25:27,080
 and the others still capture a majority

556
00:25:27,080 --> 00:25:28,920
 of the builder mind share.

557
00:25:28,920 --> 00:25:30,800
 So I think those were the key takeaways from that data.

558
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:32,360
 But you can check it out yourself

559
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:34,240
 by looking at the dashboard.

560
00:25:34,240 --> 00:25:35,280
 - Excellent.

561
00:25:35,280 --> 00:25:37,560
 So this is an election year

562
00:25:37,560 --> 00:25:42,240
 and crypto has become a part of the national conversation.

563
00:25:42,240 --> 00:25:45,320
 People are talking about it, politicians are talking about it,

564
00:25:45,320 --> 00:25:47,400
 candidates for the office of the president

565
00:25:47,400 --> 00:25:49,240
 are talking about it.

566
00:25:49,240 --> 00:25:54,240
 What have we found about how crypto has sort of changed

567
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:57,360
 from last year when we did the report to this year

568
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:00,920
 when it comes to a policy perspective?

569
00:26:00,920 --> 00:26:04,080
 - Yeah. So crypto has become political in this sense

570
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:08,120
 that the crypto industry believes that political engagements

571
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:11,880
 can help resolve some of its major challenges.

572
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:15,440
 And a big question is whether that has made it

573
00:26:15,440 --> 00:26:16,840
 to the mainstream or not.

574
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:20,000
 Have the crypto industry's issues become something

575
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:24,600
 that some voters or enough voters care about?

576
00:26:24,600 --> 00:26:26,640
 - Probably for me, the most interesting slide

577
00:26:26,640 --> 00:26:28,640
 in the policy section of this report

578
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:33,000
 is this analysis of the crypto interest

579
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:37,040
 that we're seeing within various states of the union.

580
00:26:37,040 --> 00:26:39,560
 Walk us through this analysis that you did here

581
00:26:39,560 --> 00:26:41,000
 and what you found.

582
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,960
 - I think everybody knows that this presidential election

583
00:26:43,960 --> 00:26:46,040
 is shaping up to be a very tight race.

584
00:26:46,040 --> 00:26:48,840
 I think it will likely be decided by a handful of counties

585
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:50,800
 in a handful of swing states.

586
00:26:50,800 --> 00:26:53,720
 And so we wanted to at least understand

587
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:57,280
 is the interest based on some of the data that we have,

588
00:26:57,280 --> 00:27:01,080
 is it growing or shrinking across these different states?

589
00:27:01,080 --> 00:27:04,400
 And so we basically used Google Trends data.

590
00:27:04,400 --> 00:27:07,280
 We looked at a basket of search terms

591
00:27:07,280 --> 00:27:08,520
 that were relevant to crypto.

592
00:27:08,520 --> 00:27:12,080
 So we looked at Bitcoin, Ethereum and crypto.

593
00:27:12,080 --> 00:27:16,200
 And we looked specifically at the relative change

594
00:27:16,200 --> 00:27:19,760
 in search interest as defined by Google Trends

595
00:27:19,760 --> 00:27:22,400
 from 2020 to 2024.

596
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:25,040
 And we looked at which ones increased in rank

597
00:27:25,040 --> 00:27:27,720
 and which states decreased in rank.

598
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:30,280
 And then we basically plotted that on a map

599
00:27:30,280 --> 00:27:32,720
 and showed which ones had the highest growth,

600
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:34,960
 which once had the highest declines

601
00:27:34,960 --> 00:27:37,720
 and then basically plotted all of that data.

602
00:27:37,720 --> 00:27:40,320
 And I think if we look at the swing states specifically,

603
00:27:40,320 --> 00:27:42,800
 I think the most interesting takeaway is that

604
00:27:42,800 --> 00:27:46,560
 Pennsylvania and Wisconsin actually had top five.

605
00:27:46,560 --> 00:27:49,280
 I think there were fourth and fifth in the ranks

606
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:53,600
 by change in this relative crypto related search interest

607
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:54,720
 since 2020.

608
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:57,600
 And these are some of the tightest races currently

609
00:27:57,600 --> 00:28:00,720
 as indicated by various prediction markets.

610
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:04,280
 And so I think there is many different factors at play

611
00:28:04,280 --> 00:28:07,000
 and you need to be careful in what you conclude

612
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:10,400
 from a very simple analysis like this alone.

613
00:28:10,400 --> 00:28:12,240
 But I think there is some evidence to suggest

614
00:28:12,240 --> 00:28:17,280
 that crypto is relevant here and could have an impact.

615
00:28:17,280 --> 00:28:19,160
 - Yeah, I mean, it's not a sentiment analysis.

616
00:28:19,160 --> 00:28:21,520
 So we don't know exactly what those people

617
00:28:21,520 --> 00:28:24,000
 who are searching for these crypto terms

618
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:25,480
 actually think about crypto.

619
00:28:25,480 --> 00:28:29,200
 But we do know that they are searching for crypto terms

620
00:28:29,200 --> 00:28:31,520
 which just kind of indicates general interest

621
00:28:31,520 --> 00:28:35,840
 and is pretty interesting to note in and of itself.

622
00:28:35,840 --> 00:28:38,560
 You mentioned Pennsylvania and Wisconsin in the top five.

623
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:40,020
 Michigan is I believe number eight.

624
00:28:40,020 --> 00:28:42,160
 So that's in the top 10.

625
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:44,040
 And then there were some declines.

626
00:28:44,040 --> 00:28:46,840
 Some states saw a little bit of a drop in interest.

627
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:48,560
 One of those is Nevada,

628
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:51,720
 a place that has the city of Las Vegas,

629
00:28:51,720 --> 00:28:53,200
 which is often associated with gambling.

630
00:28:53,200 --> 00:28:57,960
 So if there's less crypto interest in Nevada,

631
00:28:57,960 --> 00:29:00,640
 I don't know that that's necessarily a bad thing.

632
00:29:00,640 --> 00:29:02,360
 But it is really interesting to see that, yeah,

633
00:29:02,360 --> 00:29:05,920
 this election could come down to a few counties

634
00:29:05,920 --> 00:29:07,960
 in a few states and these are places

635
00:29:07,960 --> 00:29:11,080
 where there is increasing interest in crypto,

636
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:14,440
 especially since last election in 2020.

637
00:29:14,440 --> 00:29:16,160
 - And just to add clarifying detail,

638
00:29:16,160 --> 00:29:18,320
 all of this data is by default

639
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:20,640
 from Google Trends population adjusted.

640
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:24,360
 So they are representing this data as a percentage

641
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:27,240
 of the total searches in the region.

642
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:28,800
 We don't actually have visibility

643
00:29:28,800 --> 00:29:32,680
 into the actual total numbers by state.

644
00:29:32,680 --> 00:29:33,520
 - That's right.

645
00:29:33,520 --> 00:29:35,760
 And total numbers anyway would be sort of skewed

646
00:29:35,760 --> 00:29:39,280
 just toward the states that have the biggest populations,

647
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:42,040
 which wouldn't be extremely informative.

648
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:43,560
 Another really big thing that happened this year

649
00:29:43,560 --> 00:29:46,680
 from a policy perspective was the Bitcoin

650
00:29:46,680 --> 00:29:50,400
 and Ethereum exchange traded products were approved.

651
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:53,960
 And I'm deliberately calling them exchange traded products,

652
00:29:53,960 --> 00:29:58,080
 ETPs and not ETFs because ETFs,

653
00:29:58,080 --> 00:30:00,360
 while they are a form of ETP,

654
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:03,640
 the underlying portfolios of them would have securities.

655
00:30:03,640 --> 00:30:07,760
 Whereas in this case, these crypto ETPs were registered

656
00:30:07,760 --> 00:30:09,840
 with the SEC in a way that indicates

657
00:30:09,840 --> 00:30:11,600
 the underlying portfolios are not securities.

658
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:13,320
 Anyway, that's a really important point,

659
00:30:13,320 --> 00:30:17,320
 but it is the first time we've ever had these market products

660
00:30:17,320 --> 00:30:20,880
 accessible on major stock markets.

661
00:30:20,880 --> 00:30:22,560
 It's kind of a big deal.

662
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:23,760
 - I think it's a very big deal.

663
00:30:23,760 --> 00:30:26,640
 I think it legitimizes crypto as an asset class.

664
00:30:26,640 --> 00:30:29,800
 I think it puts us on a path to really having crypto

665
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:32,400
 be a part of every diversified portfolio

666
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:34,280
 and then just makes it much more accessible,

667
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:36,200
 which I think is a good thing.

668
00:30:36,200 --> 00:30:40,440
 In the report, we do look at these kind of net inflow numbers

669
00:30:40,440 --> 00:30:42,240
 because crypto is all on chain.

670
00:30:42,240 --> 00:30:46,560
 We can see when the ETFs are accumulating

671
00:30:46,560 --> 00:30:50,000
 and distributing crypto out of these products,

672
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:53,400
 they haven't been all that high relative to expectations.

673
00:30:53,400 --> 00:30:56,120
 Maybe it has underwhelmed a bit,

674
00:30:56,120 --> 00:30:57,440
 but I have talked to some experts

675
00:30:57,440 --> 00:31:00,280
 and they do say that getting the distributors activated

676
00:31:00,280 --> 00:31:02,640
 is just something that's gonna take time.

677
00:31:02,640 --> 00:31:05,360
 You need to go through a long process.

678
00:31:05,360 --> 00:31:07,240
 You need to get it into the model portfolios.

679
00:31:07,240 --> 00:31:09,200
 You need to discuss it with your clients.

680
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:12,200
 And I think that could be a multi-year process.

681
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:14,440
 And so I'd keep an eye on those inflow numbers

682
00:31:14,440 --> 00:31:15,880
 over the next few years

683
00:31:15,880 --> 00:31:17,160
 because now that we have them,

684
00:31:17,160 --> 00:31:20,440
 I think a tremendous tailwind for the industry.

685
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:22,960
 - Yeah, I think it absolutely does broaden access

686
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:24,440
 to a whole new segment of investors

687
00:31:24,440 --> 00:31:27,680
 that otherwise would not have dabbled with crypto beforehand.

688
00:31:27,680 --> 00:31:29,160
 I'm thinking about my dad for one.

689
00:31:29,160 --> 00:31:30,520
 He's like, where's the ETF?

690
00:31:30,520 --> 00:31:32,920
 Where's the ETP as it were?

691
00:31:32,920 --> 00:31:35,600
 'Cause he's probably not gonna set up a hardware wallet.

692
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:36,440
 All right, okay.

693
00:31:36,440 --> 00:31:38,600
 Anyway, so let's dig into some of the other things

694
00:31:38,600 --> 00:31:40,000
 we found in this report.

695
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:41,120
 People always ask this question,

696
00:31:41,120 --> 00:31:42,960
 where are the crypto applications?

697
00:31:42,960 --> 00:31:45,200
 But there's one that's kind of been staring people

698
00:31:45,200 --> 00:31:47,840
 on the face for a long time.

699
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:50,160
 And that is stablecoins.

700
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:55,160
 Stablecoins are a product that people are using a lot.

701
00:31:55,160 --> 00:31:57,840
 It's a crypto product.

702
00:31:57,840 --> 00:31:59,440
 I think people discount that.

703
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:03,040
 - Well, stablecoins have been in crypto for a long time.

704
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:05,200
 In the earlier days, people used to think of them

705
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:09,040
 as something that would end up being for payments.

706
00:32:09,040 --> 00:32:14,040
 And very interestingly, what we found in 2021, 2022,

707
00:32:14,520 --> 00:32:18,240
 as the stablecoin supply has really started to pop off,

708
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:20,400
 was that they were primarily being used

709
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:24,120
 as a means of settlement between centralized exchanges

710
00:32:24,120 --> 00:32:28,280
 and also as collateral for DeFi protocols,

711
00:32:28,280 --> 00:32:31,120
 something people could supply and lend in a protocol

712
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:34,080
 and then enable other people to borrow

713
00:32:34,080 --> 00:32:37,200
 and create debts denominated in dollars,

714
00:32:37,200 --> 00:32:38,960
 which is a lot easier to reason about

715
00:32:38,960 --> 00:32:42,400
 than a debt denominated in Ethereum

716
00:32:42,400 --> 00:32:44,760
 or some other volatile asset.

717
00:32:44,760 --> 00:32:47,120
 Another reason why stablecoins make a lot of sense

718
00:32:47,120 --> 00:32:49,720
 as collateral is that if you're borrowing

719
00:32:49,720 --> 00:32:52,120
 a fair amount on chain,

720
00:32:52,120 --> 00:32:56,520
 it may be totally reasonable to pay a 30, 50

721
00:32:56,520 --> 00:33:00,920
 or even $100 transaction fee to perform that borrow

722
00:33:00,920 --> 00:33:04,040
 because it still may be meaningfully more affordable

723
00:33:04,040 --> 00:33:08,480
 than other ways to engage in an over collateralized loan.

724
00:33:08,480 --> 00:33:12,280
 And that is really the type of use case that we saw

725
00:33:12,280 --> 00:33:17,280
 for people in 2021 through 2023 period for stablecoins.

726
00:33:17,280 --> 00:33:22,760
 But as a result of the infrastructural improvements in 2024,

727
00:33:22,760 --> 00:33:26,360
 where lots has come online, not just 4844,

728
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:31,080
 but the proliferation of L2s in Ethereum world generally.

729
00:33:31,080 --> 00:33:33,720
 - 4844 being the Denkoon upgrade

730
00:33:33,720 --> 00:33:36,920
 that Ethereum went through in March of this year,

731
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:38,440
 also called Protodanksharding.

732
00:33:38,440 --> 00:33:41,640
 - Yeah, so as a result of those infrastructural improvements,

733
00:33:41,640 --> 00:33:45,800
 as well as increasing adoption in newer generation,

734
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:49,640
 newer architecture or blockchains like Solana,

735
00:33:49,640 --> 00:33:51,720
 Sui, many others.

736
00:33:51,720 --> 00:33:55,920
 Now sending stablecoins in a single transaction

737
00:33:55,920 --> 00:33:59,080
 often costs a penny or less.

738
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:01,720
 So because of this, so just this year,

739
00:34:01,720 --> 00:34:04,640
 because of these improvements,

740
00:34:04,640 --> 00:34:06,760
 I think a lot of people are talking about stablecoins

741
00:34:06,760 --> 00:34:09,040
 as a medium for payment again.

742
00:34:09,040 --> 00:34:09,960
 It's really interesting to me

743
00:34:09,960 --> 00:34:14,360
 because I can't emphasize enough how in 2022,

744
00:34:14,360 --> 00:34:15,920
 this was just a non-start.

745
00:34:15,920 --> 00:34:16,960
 Everyone had given up on it.

746
00:34:16,960 --> 00:34:19,720
 I don't think I heard the word payment in 2022,

747
00:34:19,720 --> 00:34:22,320
 except to debunk the possibility

748
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:25,200
 that stablecoins were being used for payments.

749
00:34:25,200 --> 00:34:28,160
 Whereas now it's something that I'm hearing about all the time

750
00:34:28,160 --> 00:34:31,240
 and it's not just in a conventional pay for your coffee

751
00:34:31,240 --> 00:34:33,720
 with crypto setting, although that is actually

752
00:34:33,720 --> 00:34:37,240
 a real possibility Coinbase had a pay for a coffee

753
00:34:37,240 --> 00:34:39,600
 with the USDC day in New York, not long ago.

754
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:42,560
 It was a lot of fun, but more concretely,

755
00:34:42,560 --> 00:34:45,760
 now people can play with blockchains

756
00:34:45,760 --> 00:34:47,800
 as a piece of infrastructure

757
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:52,120
 to potentially bring down interchange fees

758
00:34:52,120 --> 00:34:55,360
 and merchant network fees, all the types of fees

759
00:34:55,360 --> 00:34:59,560
 that go into settling an actual consumer payment

760
00:34:59,560 --> 00:35:00,400
 for something.

761
00:35:00,400 --> 00:35:03,160
 I just pre-ordered a book from Stripe Press

762
00:35:03,160 --> 00:35:06,440
 with the new Stripe Pay with Crypto feature.

763
00:35:06,440 --> 00:35:10,160
 USDC on Solana was fantastic, it was just a really straightforward

764
00:35:10,160 --> 00:35:11,000
 experience for me.

765
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,120
 Of course, I already have crypto on my wallet,

766
00:35:13,120 --> 00:35:17,880
 so that made it pretty convenient, but these are real things.

767
00:35:17,880 --> 00:35:20,000
 Right, this is because you've got to still onboard

768
00:35:20,000 --> 00:35:24,840
 to like a layer two network, say, an L2, a roll up,

769
00:35:24,840 --> 00:35:26,400
 where the fees would be lower.

770
00:35:26,400 --> 00:35:28,440
 But once you're there, it becomes quite seamless

771
00:35:28,440 --> 00:35:30,480
 to transact and move around.

772
00:35:30,480 --> 00:35:33,520
 Yeah, and there's still very meaningful UX challenges.

773
00:35:33,520 --> 00:35:36,120
 But the point is to say is like, now this is in the story

774
00:35:36,120 --> 00:35:37,720
 and there's a lot of crypto projects

775
00:35:37,720 --> 00:35:40,160
 that are interested in trying to improve payments

776
00:35:40,160 --> 00:35:42,440
 or aspects of payments or take advantage

777
00:35:42,440 --> 00:35:46,200
 of the ease of settling payments internationally,

778
00:35:46,200 --> 00:35:47,880
 computationally.

779
00:35:47,880 --> 00:35:50,320
 There's all kinds of ways that payments really ought

780
00:35:50,320 --> 00:35:52,440
 to be programmed and experimented with.

781
00:35:52,440 --> 00:35:54,560
 And now just this year, that's a possibility.

782
00:35:54,560 --> 00:35:56,960
 So just to set people's expectations like the way

783
00:35:56,960 --> 00:36:00,280
 I like to think about it is now that there is a glut

